 "result": "**Where it's decided:** `src/tessel/wrappers.py:59-86`. The `Request.max_content_length` property returns, in order:\n\n1. `self._max_content_length`, if it has been set on the request.\n2. Otherwise `active_app.config[\"MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH\"]`, which defaults to `None` (no limit). Without an active app it falls back to `super().max_content_length`.\n\n**Per request:** yes. The property has a setter (line 88-90), which was added in 3.1 per the docstring and `CHANGES.rst:83`. In the one view that needs a bigger limit, set it before the body is read:\n\n```python\n@app.post(\"/big-upload\")\ndef big_upload():\n    request.max_content_length = 100 * 1024 * 1024  # 100 MB\n    f = request.files[\"file\"]\n    ...\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Set it before anything accesses `request.files`, `request.form`, `request.data` or `request.get_json()`. The 413 is raised when the body is first read, so setting it afterwards has no effect.\n- A per-request value overrides the global `MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH`. If the global limit is what's rejecting the upload, you can raise it for that one view this way without changing the config.\n- `max_form_memory_size` and `max_form_parts` (lines 56-57) work the same way. If large multipart forms still fail, check those too.",
